    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d243">

                <pb facs="0380" n="208" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp01v01/pg208"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111Y2/7</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gerard</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-01-26T11:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 26, 1917, 11 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received January 27, 7 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4929. One of correspondents here learned yesterday from an Undersecretary of
                    State that there were some Americans among the <pb facs="0381" n="209"
                        xml:id="pg209"/> 107 neutrals brought to Swinemünde on <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Yarrowdale</hi>, and that there was a big row on as to
                    what position should be taken by Government with reference to them. I sent notes
                    to Foreign Office requesting information as to whether Americans were among
                    these prisoners on January 20, 24, 25, and to-day, but no answer.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gerard</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
